     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p48-investigation-explanation",
     "replacement": "**Reversing the chain rule** means identifying the **inner function** and its **derivative** in the integrand.\n\nYou **can adjust for a missing constant**. A constant scales every output by the same amount, so you can move it outside the integral.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int x^2(x^3+5)^7\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{1}{24}\\int24x^2(x^3+5)^7\\,dx\\end{aligned}$ ✓\n\nYou **cannot adjust for a missing variable factor**. A factor that depends on $x$ does not scale every output by the same amount, so you cannot move it outside the integral.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int(x^3+5)^7\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{1}{24x^2}\\int24x^2(x^3+5)^7\\,dx\\end{aligned}$** ✗ Invalid**\n\nSo you cannot use the reverse chain rule directly on $\\int(x^3+5)^7\\,dx$, because the integrand does not contain the inner derivative $3x^2$. Expand it and integrate term by term instead.\n\n**Constant factors can move outside an integral; factors that depend on the variable of integration cannot.**",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p48-investigation-explanation",
     "targetFingerprint": "dad621c48e7c60678e341cfcf936f75cb7e2bf3f2d07c35dec009b4ee80fc14e",
